# Foundry usage for AI assistants

Foundry keeps project context under `~/.foundry/`. The **crate ships a normal CLI** (`foundry`); there is **no in-repo MCP server**. Prefer **reading and editing files** (including via a `.foundry` symlink) over expecting JSON-RPC tools.

## Principles

- **Structure, not authorship**: `foundry project init` / `foundry spec init` create **empty** markdown; you fill `vision.md`, specs, etc. with your editor tools.
- **Content-agnostic**: Foundry does not score or rewrite your prose; it validates names/paths and manages directories.
- **Kebab-case**: Project and feature names for `init` commands use kebab-case (e.g. `my-app`, `auth-flow`).

## Commands (quick reference)

| Goal | Command |
|------|---------|
| New project scaffold | `foundry project init <name>` |
| Print project files | `foundry project load <name>` |
| List projects | `foundry project list` |
| Remove project | `foundry project delete <name> --confirm` |
| New spec scaffold | `foundry spec init <project> <feature>` |
| Print spec files | `foundry spec load <project> <id-or-partial>` |
| List specs | `foundry spec list <project>` |
| Remove spec | `foundry spec delete <project> <id> --confirm` |
| Symlink `~/.foundry/<p>` → `./.foundry` | `foundry link [project]` |
| Git backup of store | `foundry git init`, `foundry git remote <url>`, `foundry git sync <msg...>` |
| Install bundled skills | `foundry install claude-code` / `foundry install cursor` |

## Workflow in a repo

1. `foundry project init …` (once per logical project in the store).
2. `foundry link …` in the codebase so `.foundry/` points at that store entry.
3. Edit `.foundry/vision.md`, specs under `.foundry/specs/…`, etc.
4. Optional: `foundry git sync "…"` when using git backup on `~/.foundry/`.

## Skills

Bundled skills (`foundry:load`, `foundry:new`, `foundry:done`) are installed as `.md` files by `foundry install`; they describe this workflow for Claude/Cursor. See `assets/skills/` and `README.md`.

## Do not assume

- No `create_project` / `update_spec` MCP tool calls—those APIs are gone.
- No `foundry serve` in current versions.
